### How to build your trees
- **No worktree is created in the NexusAI repo, and you never work in its `2_Project_Files` checkout or any builder `worktrees/` directory** (S86P's, S87N's `worktrees/s84n-rd424`, S86M's `worktrees/s86m-rd735`). In that repo use ONLY read verbs: `show`, `diff`, `log`, `ls-tree`, `cat-file`, `grep`, `ls-remote`, `rev-parse`, `merge-base`, `rev-list` (plus `count-objects` for §9.9). **NEVER `fetch`, `pull`, `push`, `checkout`, `worktree`, `commit`, `stash`, `gc`, `merge`; and `merge-tree --write-tree` ONLY with `GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY=<your own scratch dir> GIT_ALTERNATE_OBJECT_DIRECTORIES=<repo>/.git/objects`.**
- **Every tree is K1** — `git clone --shared --no-checkout <repo> <dir>` then `checkout` in YOUR clone (origin removed; local identity; `gc.auto 0`; `core.fsmonitor false`; hooks off), under a fresh `mktemp -d` in `/Volumes/KK_T9_External_HDD/!CODING/Testing Agent MAIN/projects/nexusai/qa-trees/batch15.XXXXXX/`. Status-check before use.
- **Each tree is EXCLUSIVE to this gate and to ONE purpose.** A fresh arm per mutant; never reuse a mutant tree for a clean arm; `batch15`-prefixed directories only. **Gate 14's trees (`qa-trees/batch14.*`) are a LIVE peer's — never run in them, never write to them, never clone node_modules FROM them** (nor from gate 12's or 13's).
- **The red-at-parent trees:** **A:** YOUR clone at `0cb584d` (the head's cells on `3b6c9ec`'s page, no vendored file, no record) — and a clone at `7861a06` with ONLY `static/index.html` restored to `25b7bd8`. **B:** YOUR clone at `cca852c` with `backend/jsonStorage.js` restored to `a63d862` (round 1's product = `08655ee`) — and a second with it restored to M0's `296e78f`. **SLOT:** YOUR clone at `7e2cc9f` with `backend/services/cspReport.js` restored to `6608eae`. Each hashed after the restore, committed IN YOUR CLONE ONLY so each tree is a clean K1.
- **`node_modules` — ONE lockfile for the members (`e9063d4`); a second (`9064763`) only if the SLOT joins.** Each tree runs on node_modules PROVEN for its own lock (`npm ls --all --offline` rc 0; every `invalid`/`extraneous`/`missing` line quoted; axios, http-cache-semantics, sqlite3 and **playwright 1.62.1** read back). **Source (H-31):** for each lock ONCE, `npm ci --offline --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund --cache "<your mktemp dir>/npm-cache"` in YOUR OWN K1 tree, the `_cacache` an APFS clone (`cp -c -R`) of `~/.npm/_cacache`; **then sqlite3's binding from an APFS clone of `~/.npm/_prebuilds` by sqlite3's own `prebuild-install` inside the strict belt, the log saying "found cached prebuild … unpacking", and a load proof** (ruling m, its three conditions). An `ENOTCACHED` is NOT RUN for that tree — name it and mail a QUESTION; **never go online.** Every other tree gets node_modules by an APFS clone of one of YOUR proven trees. A real directory, never a symlink. **Never `npm install`.** Say that `--ignore-scripts` differs from CI's form (L-Y1).

## 2. Why these tiers, and who is waiting
- **RD-719 TIER 1 — severity rules:** **vendored bytes that differ from the bytes the root-commit integrity pin names is a Blocker; a shipped page loading Chart.js from any remote host, or the integrity attribute dropped or not enforced by a real browser, is a Blocker; the image's file set changing beyond `+static/vendor/chart-3.9.1.min.js` or a shipped blob other than `static/index.html` changing is a Major; the charts failing to draw offline in a real local render of the head (open mode OR signed in) is a Major; a new CSP source is a Major; a swap of file + record + attribute that no cell catches is a Major (C-40: the identity is guarded by the record alone); the licence text not shipping is a FINDING whose severity Tuesday rules (L-A5); a READY claim that is false is a Minor.**
- **RD-424 TIER 1 — severity rules:** **a named store's pre-erasure bytes in ANY new copy, or a named store restored, at the head or on MT1, is a Blocker (R-1); an unnamed store left frozen (not backed up, or not restored) when the ledger is traceable, or booting EMPTY after a tear in r8's shapes on a real server, is a Major (D-F2 not closed); a `purging` or untraceable ledger that narrows the freeze is a Major (shape (1)/(2)); restore not failing CLOSED, or rotation not failing OPEN, on an unreadable record is a Major; a ledger label shape M0's purge writes that the head misattributes to the WRONG store is a Blocker, and one it cannot trace is the accepted cost (L-B4) — name it; D5 green for a reason other than its two asserts is a Major (the slip's class); a C-68 cell that passed at M0 failing on MT1 is a Major; a READY claim that is false is a Minor.**
- **RD-735 round 2 (SLOT) TIER 2 — severity rules:** **the f4 ladder still super-linear at the head (any doubling > ~3× above the noise floor) is a Major; a timing cell that cannot fail (L1/L2 green with the quadratic trim restored) is a Major; a claimed U1 shape keeping userinfo is a Minor each; an ordinary value changed beyond the named `mailto:` case is a Minor; a READY claim that is false is a Minor.**
- **Who is waiting:** RD-719's merge author is **NexusAI-P (S86P)** — and **RD-721 (lane 4) cannot start until this gate returns GO** (C-182 ADDENDUM); RD-424's is **NexusAI-N (S87N)** (RD-747, RD-420, RD-744 hold the out-of-scope findings); the SLOT's is **NexusAI-M (S86M)**, behind RD-618.

